	<center><h3>Recipe</h3></center>
	<u><strong>Murgh do Pyaza</strong></u>
	<ul>
	<u>Ingredients</u>
  <li>Heat oil in a pan and add bay leaves and dried red chillies.</li>
  <li>Add chopped onions and cook until golden brown.</li>
  <li>Add chopped garlic and ginger, sauté for a minute.</li>
  <li>Add chilli powder, turmeric, cumin powder, and coriander powder. Mix well.</li>
  <li>Add tomato puree and cook until oil separates.</li>
  <li>Add whisked yoghurt and stir continuously.</li>
  <li>Add chicken pieces and mix well with the masala.</li>
  <li>Add water and salt, then bring to a boil.</li>
  <li>Add potatoes, cover, and cook until chicken and potatoes are tender.</li>
  <li>Sprinkle garam masala and mix.</li>
  <li>Garnish with fresh coriander and serve.</li>
		</ul>
	
<ol><u>
step-by-steps</u>
  <li>Grind garlic and ginger with a splash of water to make a paste.</li>
  <li>Mix the paste with chicken in a bowl and marinate for 1 hour or overnight.</li>
  <li>Heat oil in a heavy-bottom pan over medium heat.</li>
  <li>Add bay leaves and dried chillies, fry for a few seconds.</li>
  <li>Add onions and fry for 17–18 minutes until browned.</li>
  <li>Add marinated chicken and cook on slightly high heat for 7–8 minutes to seal.</li>
  <li>Add chilli powder, turmeric, cumin, and coriander powder; cook for 1 minute.</li>
  <li>Add tomato puree, mix well, and cook for 4 minutes.</li>
  <li>Lower heat and add yoghurt gradually, stirring continuously for 6 minutes.</li>
  <li>Add water, potatoes, and salt; cover and cook on low heat for 20 minutes.</li>
  <li>Stir halfway through, then turn off heat and add garam masala; let it rest.</li>
  <li>In another pan, heat oil and fry shallots for 5–6 minutes per side until slightly charred.</li>
  <li>Add fried shallots to the curry and mix well.</li>
  <li>Add a splash of water if needed to adjust consistency.</li>
  <li>Garnish with coriander and lemon juice; serve with rice or roti.</li>
</ol>
  
